.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt FALLOCATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fallocate
.Nd reserve a file's space, which no file system here does
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn fallocate "int fd" "int mode" "off_t offset" "off_t len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On Linux
.Fn fallocate
reserves the blocks of a range of a file ahead of the writes to it.
No file system of this kernel reserves blocks ahead of a write, as Linux's
ext2 does not, so a call on a regular file open for writing fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
Linux's answer for a file system without the operation, whatever
.Fa mode
is.
glibc's
.Fn posix_fallocate
then writes the range itself, as it does on Linux's ext2; it does not
after
.Er ENOSYS ,
the error the call gave before it was registered.
musl's
.Fn posix_fallocate
returns the error.
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 324, with the descriptor in EBX, the mode in
ECX, the offset in EDX and ESI and the length in EDI and EBP, each low word
first.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The call returns -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
requires
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
of a file open for writing,
.Er EBADF
of one open only for reading,
.Er EINVAL
of a length of 0, and
.Er ESPIPE
of a pipe.
.Sh ERRORS
The checks are made in Linux's order:
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa offset
is negative or
.Fa len
is not positive.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open for writing.
.It Bq Er ESPIPE
.Fa fd
is a pipe.
.It Bq Er EISDIR
.Fa fd
is a directory.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
.Fa fd
is neither a regular file nor one of the above.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa fd
is a regular file open for writing.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
.Xr write 2
